<p>British Prime Minister Keir Starmer faced growing pressure from within the governing Labour Party after heavy losses in local elections across England and in legislative votes in Scotland and Wales, with more than 70 Labour lawmakers publicly urging him to resign or set a timetable for leaving office. Four government aides resigned as calls for him to go widened, and several reports said Cabinet ministers including Home Secretary Shabana Mahmood and Foreign Secretary Yvette Cooper had urged Starmer to consider an orderly departure. Starmer said in a speech in London on Monday that he would not step aside. He pledged a bolder programme that included rebuilding relations with the European Union and putting Britain “at the heart of Europe”. He warned that a Labour leadership contest would create “chaos” and said the party should not become “a weaker version” of Reform UK or the Greens, which gained support as Labour lost votes from both the right and the left. A Cabinet meeting was set for Tuesday, with senior ministers split over whether Starmer should remain in office. Senior minister Darren Jones said the Prime Minister was “listening to colleagues” and would decide whether to say more.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/4B0yKyu5gP8_We3V4nDx8ES78z6DEao19oUU79UlUOAu5K6L0wnQ3uIOB-GlnwAx_XQ75EKu1RVnRUQusXxhacP5vR3rY-_Bu_-GGvFPqVs5hW0-VQDhgmEzsv--WcSLwm4fUw0i0Lk7_9oONQgZdTWy04BvPx6qD4yszAuiHkcVrLVoCjnLaubsyIlXHuJnNG0' alt='Prime Minister Keir Starmer in London on Monday. “I get it. I feel it. And I take responsibility,” he said of last week’s results.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Possible challengers: Angela Rayner, Wes Streeting and Greater Manchester Mayor Andy Burnham have been discussed as potential successors, but none had publicly called for Starmer to resign in the cited reports.</li><li>Backbench trigger: Catherine West said she wanted a leadership election by September and had sought support to force Starmer to set out a departure timetable.</li><li>Policy pressure: Reports cited public anger over stalled growth, strained public services, the cost of living, welfare policy reversals and Starmer’s appointment of Peter Mandelson as ambassador to Washington as factors in the pressure on him.</li></ul><blockquote>I know I have my doubters and I know I need to prove them wrong, and I will - Keir Starmer</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Keir Starmer: Starmer acknowledged voter frustration with politics and said some people were disappointed in him, but said his government had to show people “tired of a status quo” that it was on their side. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/as-he-faces-calls-to-step-down-starmer-pledges-to-bring-britain-closer-to-eu'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>Darren Jones: Jones said ministers were focused on their jobs and on delivering promised policies while Starmer considered concerns raised by colleagues. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2026/may/12/keir-starmer-prime-minister-resignation-labour-leadership-cabinet-meeting-yvette-cooper-shabana-mahmood-andy-burnham-wes-streeting-uk-politics-latest-news-updates'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Tom Rutland: Rutland, who resigned as a ministerial aide, said Starmer had lost authority within the parliamentary Labour Party and across the country. (<a href='https://tribune.com.pk/story/2607614/pressure-grows-on-starmer-to-quit'>The Express Tribune</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://japantoday.com/category/world/starmer-pledges-to-bring-britain-closer-to-the-eu-as-he-fights-calls-for-his-ouster'>Starmer pledges to prove his doubters wrong but faces wave of resignation calls</a> - japantoday.com</li><li><a href='https://www.boston.com/news/politics/2026/05/11/british-prime-minister-starmer-pledges-to-prove-his-doubters-wrong-but-faces-a-wave-of-resignation-calls/'>British Prime Minister Starmer pledges to prove his doubters wrong but faces a wave of resignation calls</a> - boston.com</li><li><a hr